!PlexRO - a Plex client for RISC OS                  0.1.0-test4
===================================================================

PlexRO browses the films and TV programmes on a Plex Media Server and
plays them in ReelEGL or Reel, the video players from riscos-ffmpeg.
Files the players can play as they are go to the player directly.
Everything else is converted by the server, as it plays, into a stream
the players can handle: H.264 video with stereo AAC sound.

This is a test build.

PlexRO is a working name. Plex is a trademark of Plex, Inc.; PlexRO isn't
made by Plex or connected with it.

Starting
--------
Double-click !PlexRO: its icon (a screen with posters and a play button)
appears on the icon bar. The first time, click Select on it to sign in.

Signing in
----------
PlexRO has one window. Until you've signed in, it shows the sign-in page
with a four-letter code. On any computer or phone, go to plex.tv/link,
sign in to your Plex account there, and type the code. PlexRO checks every
2 seconds, and carries on by itself once the code has been typed: it finds
your servers and shows your libraries. PlexRO never sees your password. A
code runs out after a while: New code gets another.

A server on your own network can be used without plex.tv: click in
Address and type its address (such as 192.168.1.10; :32400 is added),
then Tab (or Return) to Token and type a token (the server's or your
account's X-Plex-Token; it's shown as dots), then Return or Use these.
Backspace deletes, Ctrl-U empties the field.

The browser
-----------
Select on the icon bar icon opens it: a dark window, like a TV's. At the
top: Back, where you are, a status line, and Refresh. Below them, a
poster for each film, show, season or episode, with its name and (for
example) its year, "S1 E3" or "3 seasons". A blue corner marks what you
haven't watched; a blue bar on a poster shows how far you got. The
selected poster has a blue ring, and the one under the pointer a grey one.

  click          select
  double-click   open a library, show or season; a video's details page
  Return         open, or play, the poster that's selected
  I              the selected video's details page
  arrow keys     move the selection
  Backspace, Escape, Delete  go back

Poster size on the icon's menu: Small, Medium (the default) or Large,
remembered.

The top list is Continue watching, then your libraries. Films and TV
libraries open; music and photo libraries aren't handled yet.

Menu on a poster:
  Play (Open)         play it (open a folder)
  Details...          its details page (below)
  Resume from h:mm:ss where Plex says you stopped. The server streams it
                      from there (copying the video when it can)
  Play from start     from the beginning
  Subtitles >         None, or one of its subtitle tracks (below)
  Save original file > the file itself, onto your disc (below)
  Test speed          how fast the server sends it (below)
  Mark watched, Mark unwatched
  Back, Refresh

The details page
----------------
Double-click a film or episode (or press I) and the window shows its
details: its backdrop across the top, its title, year, running time, age
rating and score, its summary, and how it will play. Buttons: Play,
Resume from h:mm:ss, From the start, Save file, Mark watched (or
unwatched), and its subtitles.

  Return         play
  Left, Right    the video before or after it in the list
  Back, Backspace, Escape  back to the posters, where you were

Subtitles
---------
Choose them on the Subtitles button on the details page, or Subtitles
on a poster's menu: None, or one of the video's tracks (in the file, or a
file of their own beside it on the server). The choice is kept on the
server, so the Plex apps on your other devices show the same.

For now Reel can't be told which subtitles to show, so a video with
subtitles chosen is converted by the server, which burns them into the
picture (any kind: text, DVD or Blu-ray). The status line and the details
page say so. Choose None to play it directly again.

Playing
-------
PlexRO gives the video to the player chosen on Player on the icon's menu:
ReelEGL (the default) or Reel. If it's running, it gets the video at
once; if not, PlexRO starts it. The Filer must have seen the player's
application (open the directory it's in, or add it to your boot
sequence), or PlexRO can't find it.

The status line says how it will play:
  "Direct play: ..."         the file itself: nothing is converted
  "Converted by the server"  the server converts it, and says why (for
                             example HEVC video, 10-bit video, or a bit
                             rate above the quality's limit)

Quality on the icon's menu sets the limits, for direct play and for
what the server converts to: 1080p (10 Mbit/s), 720p (4 Mbit/s, the
default) or 480p (2 Mbit/s). The Raspberry Pi 4 decodes H.264 on one
core, so the bit rate matters as much as the size. Untick Direct play
when possible to have the server convert everything.

If a video keeps stopping to buffer
-----------------------------------
Test speed, on a video's menu, reads the start of its file from the
server for 8 seconds (the desktop carries on meanwhile), then says how
fast it came, in Mbit/s, and how much the file needs:
  plenty           the server and the network keep up: the player isn't
                   reading fast enough (tell us, with the numbers)
  too slow         untick Direct play (the server then converts it to
                   the Quality's limit), choose a lower Quality, or save
                   the file first and play it from the disc
It also says whether the server is reached over http or https (https is
slower on RISC OS), and whether it's on the local network.

Saving the original file
------------------------
Move to the right of Save original file on a poster's menu: a save box
opens. Drag its icon to a directory display (or type a full path and
click Save). The file is saved a piece at a time, so the desktop carries
on; the status line shows how far it's got, and how fast (MB/s). Stop saving is on the same
menu while it's saving.

RISC OS's filing systems hold files of up to 4GB, so bigger files are
refused at once.

Choices
-------
Kept in Choices:PlexRO.Choices: the player, the quality, direct play,
the server in use, and the tokens that sign PlexRO in to plex.tv and the
server. Anyone who can read your Choices can use those tokens: Sign out
on the icon's menu removes them.

Needs
-----
- RISC OS 5 on an ARMv7 machine (Raspberry Pi 2/3/4/400 and so on).
- ReelEGL or Reel 0.1.17 or later (riscos-ffmpeg), which plays what
  PlexRO finds. Their sound needs SharedSoundBuffer and StreamManager:
  download ssb.zip from https://orac.co.uk/software/rdpclient/rdpclient.html
  and merge its !System. John Duffell's own site (now on the Internet
  Archive) has more details:
  https://web.archive.org/web/20110920080106/http://www.duffell.riscos.me.uk/
- SharedUnixLibrary 1.16 or later and ARMEABISupport (PackMan).
- AcornSSL (RISC OS 5.28 or later) for signing in with a code, and for
  servers reached through their plex.direct addresses.

!Run loads the modules it needs. PThreadTicker (UnixLib's thread timer)
is in the application directory.

Debugging: *Set PlexRO$Debug 1 before starting it lets FFmpeg's network
messages through (to see them, run !RunImage from a TaskWindow).

